1
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:04,580
Ask almost any computer to
add a tenth and two tenths,

2
00:00:04,630 --> 00:00:17,090
and it says 0.30000000000000004.
That's not a bug in your language.

3
00:00:17,140 --> 00:00:20,830
Most software stores numbers
as binary floating point,

4
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:26,080
with a fixed number of digits, and a
tenth can't be written exactly in binary.

5
00:00:26,130 --> 00:00:27,999
So it rounds.

6
00:00:29,749 --> 00:00:35,379
Times are numbers too. A computer usually
stores a moment as a count of seconds,

7
00:00:35,429 --> 00:00:41,339
or milliseconds, from the start
of 1970. And with floating point,

8
00:00:41,389 --> 00:00:45,831
the further a time is from 1970,
the coarser that count gets.

9
00:00:47,561 --> 00:00:52,461
Today, a double-precision time can tell
apart moments a quarter of a microsecond

10
00:00:52,511 --> 00:00:57,181
apart. At the dinosaurs' extinction,
sixty-six million years ago,

11
00:00:57,231 --> 00:01:01,671
its steps are a quarter of a
second. At Earth's formation,

12
00:01:01,721 --> 00:01:07,431
they're sixteen seconds: two events ten
seconds apart become the same time.

13
00:01:07,481 --> 00:01:10,301
For a timeline that
spans billions of years,

14
00:01:10,351 --> 00:01:12,357
that's not good enough.

15
00:01:13,787 --> 00:01:18,897
A rational number is a fraction:
one whole number over another.

16
00:01:18,947 --> 00:01:25,427
A tenth is one over ten, exactly. A
tenth plus two tenths is three tenths,

17
00:01:25,477 --> 00:01:28,897
exactly. A third of a
second is one over three,

18
00:01:28,947 --> 00:01:34,597
and stays exactly a third. And because
the whole numbers can be as large as they

19
00:01:34,647 --> 00:01:38,000
need to be, there's no point
where precision runs out.

20
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:44,050
OpenChronology stores every
time this way. In the editor,

21
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:47,940
in the database on the server,
and in the desktop app's files,

22
00:01:47,990 --> 00:01:52,600
a time is a reduced fraction
of arbitrary-size integers.

23
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:56,044
Saving, exporting and
importing never round it.

24
00:01:57,484 --> 00:02:02,954
So you can zoom as far as you like. Put
a moment at a third of a second and zoom

25
00:02:03,004 --> 00:02:07,454
in a billion times: it's
still exactly at a third.

26
00:02:07,504 --> 00:02:10,632
The same goes four and a
half billion years ago.

27
00:02:12,062 --> 00:02:17,492
Rounding happens only when a time is
shown. A timeline's time display reads the

28
00:02:17,542 --> 00:02:23,012
same exact value as a fraction,
in SI units, or as a date.

29
00:02:23,062 --> 00:02:29,242
A date that isn't a whole second keeps
its remainder as a fraction in braces.

30
00:02:29,292 --> 00:02:32,282
Leave a field as it is,
and the exact value stays;

31
00:02:32,332 --> 00:02:35,466
type a new time, and
you've chosen a new one.

32
00:02:37,196 --> 00:02:40,666
Billions of years, a single
second, and a third of one,

33
00:02:40,716 --> 00:02:44,876
on the same line, with no
rounding anywhere they're kept.

34
00:02:44,926 --> 00:02:49,105
It's free software, so you can
read exactly how it's done.
